<section class="hero">
  <p class="hero__eyebrow">添削 <span>· correcting a draft, for Rails</span></p>
  <h1 class="hero__title">
    Fix your Rails app
    <span class="pen-mark">on the page<svg viewBox="0 0 240 14" preserveAspectRatio="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M3 9 C 45 3, 92 12, 138 7 S 214 4, 237 8"/></svg></span>.
  </h1>
  <p class="hero__lead">
    Click any text on a running page and edit it in place. Tensaku writes the change back to the ERB
    template, the locale YAML or the Markdown file, byte for byte. Or leave a note, and your coding
    agent gets it with the exact file and line.
  </p>
  <div class="hero__actions">
    <a class="button button--ink" href="#demo">Try it below</a>
    <a class="button" href="/installation">Install it</a>
  </div>
  <p class="hero__fine">Development only. Production keeps rendering with Erubi and never loads Tensaku.</p>
</section>

<section class="demo" id="demo" data-demo data-demo-overlay="<%= asset_path("tensaku.js") %>">
  <div class="demo__intro">
    <h2>Try it on this page</h2>
    <p>
      This is Tensaku's real overlay, running on a small sample app. Here it talks to a stand-in for
      your Rails server, so the source file on the right changes in your browser instead of on disk.
    </p>
    <p class="demo__live-note">You're running the docs in development, so this is the real thing: edits are written to <code>app/views/demo/_inbox.html.erb</code>.</p>
  </div>

  <ol class="demo__steps">
    <li><strong>Alt-click</strong> (⌥-click on a Mac) the heading. Change <em>Welcome back</em>, then press Enter.</li>
    <li>Edit <em>from</em> in one message. All three change: it's one line in a loop.</li>
    <li>Press <kbd>Alt</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>T</kbd>, click a message and choose <strong>Add note</strong>.</li>
    <li>Let the agent pick it up, and watch the pin turn green.</li>
  </ol>
  <p class="demo__touch">On a touch screen, tap the 添 button in the corner, then <strong>Edit</strong>.</p>

  <div class="demo__grid">
    <figure class="demo__app">
      <div class="window-bar" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span><code>localhost:3000/inbox</code></div>
      <div class="demo__stage" data-demo-stage>
        <%= render "demo/inbox", user: @user, messages: @messages %>
      </div>
      <figcaption class="margin-note margin-note--app">your app, in development</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="demo__side" data-demo-chrome>
      <figure class="panel">
        <figcaption class="panel__title"><span class="panel__dot"></span><code>app/views/demo/_inbox.html.erb</code></figcaption>
        <pre class="demo-code" data-demo-source-code><%= File.read(Rails.root.join("app/views/demo/_inbox.html.erb")) %></pre>
      </figure>
      <figure class="panel">
        <figcaption class="panel__title">
          <span class="panel__dot panel__dot--agent"></span>What your agent reads
          <button type="button" class="button button--small" data-demo-agent-resolve disabled>Resolve as the agent</button>
        </figcaption>
        <pre class="demo-agent" data-demo-agent>No open notes. Leave one on the demo, and it shows up here the way your agent reads it.</pre>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="features">
  <article>
    <h3><span class="ink-red">Edit</span> text in place</h3>
    <p>
      The editor shows the source, never the rendered text. Ruby stays as a locked chip, so you change
      the words around <code>&lt;%= user.name %&gt;</code> and nothing else. Translations, attributes
      and Markdown blocks work the same way.
    </p>
  </article>
  <article>
    <h3><span class="ink-blue">Notes</span> with a file and line</h3>
    <p>
      Point at an element or drag over an area, and say what should change. Your agent reads the note
      through MCP, the note files or the clipboard, already knowing where to look.
    </p>
  </article>
  <article>
    <h3><span class="ink-green">Production</span> doesn't change</h3>
    <p>
      Tensaku lives in the development group. Every write is a splice of exactly the bytes you edited,
      checked against what the page was rendered from. YAML is never loaded and dumped.
    </p>
  </article>
</section>

<section class="install">
  <h2>Two commands</h2>
  <pre class="install__code"><code><span class="comment"># Gemfile</span>
group :development do
  gem "tensaku"
end</code></pre>
  <pre class="install__code"><code>bundle install
bin/rails generate tensaku:install</code></pre>
  <p>Requires Ruby 4.0 and Rails 8.1 or later. Built on <a href="https://github.com/marcoroth/herb">Herb</a> through <a href="https://github.com/marcoroth/reactionview">ReActionView</a>. <a href="/introduction">Read the docs →</a></p>
</section>
